A subarray is resilient if for every position $i$ in it, deleting element $i$ leaves a sum divisible by $k$.

Let the subarray have sum $S$. Deleting element $x$ leaves sum $S - x$. So for every element $x$ in the subarray, $S - x \equiv 0 \pmod{k}$, i.e., $x \equiv S \pmod{k}$.

So all elements in the subarray must be congruent to $S \pmod k$.

If all elements are congruent to $r \pmod k$, and there are $m$ elements, then $S \equiv mr \pmod k$. We need $r \equiv S \equiv mr \pmod k$, so $(m-1)r \equiv 0 \pmod k$.

So the condition is: all elements in the subarray have the same residue $r \pmod k$, and $(m-1)r \equiv 0 \pmod k$ where $m$ is the length.

Wait, but also each element must equal $S$ mod $k$. If all elements are $\equiv r$, then each element $x \equiv r$ and we need $x \equiv S$, so $r \equiv S \pmod k$. $S \equiv mr$, so we need $mr \equiv r$, i.e., $(m-1)r \equiv 0 \pmod k$.

So a subarray is resilient iff:
- All elements have the same residue $r \pmod k$.
- $(m-1) \cdot r \equiv 0 \pmod k$ where $m$ is the subarray length.

We want the longest such subarray. Since $n \le 2500$, $O(n^2)$ should be fine (6.25M operations).

Brute force: for each starting index $i$, expand $j$ from $i$ onward, keeping track of residue consistency. Actually if we fix residue $r$ as nums[i] % k, then expand j while nums[j] % k == r, and check $(m-1)*r \% k == 0$ where $m = j-i+1$. Record max length.

That's $O(n^2)$ with early break when residue changes. Fine.

